examples.question_text:
A parabola has an $x$-intercept at $x=-4$ and axis of symmetry $x=-1$, as shown. Use symmetry to find the other $x$-intercept.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=5mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[<->,black] (-5.5,0)--(3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->,black] (0,-4.4)--(0,3.2) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed,black] (-1,-4.2)--(-1,3);
\path (-1,2.8) node[anchor=east] {$x=-1$};
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<->,smooth,variable=\x,domain=-5:3,samples=80] plot ({\x},{0.4*(\x+4)*(\x-2)});
\fill[black] (-4,0) circle (1.8pt);
\path (-4,0) node[above left] {$-4$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The intercept $x=-4$ is $3$ units left of the axis $x=-1$.
The other intercept is $3$ units right of the axis.
$\begin{align*}x&=-1+3\\&=2\end{align*}$
The other $x$-intercept is $(2,0)$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=5mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\definecolor{curveBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[<->,black] (-5.5,0)--(3.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[<->,black] (0,-4.4)--(0,3.2) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed,black] (-1,-4.2)--(-1,3);
\path (-1,2.8) node[anchor=east] {$x=-1$};
\draw[thick,curveBlue,<->,smooth,variable=\x,domain=-5:3,samples=80] plot ({\x},{0.4*(\x+4)*(\x-2)});
\fill[black] (-4,0) circle (1.8pt);
\fill[curveBlue] (2,0) circle (1.8pt);
\path (-4,0) node[above left] {$-4$};
\path (2,0) node[above right,text=curveBlue] {$2$};
\draw[<->,black] (-4,0.8)--(-1,0.8);
\draw[<->,black] (-1,0.8)--(2,0.8);
\path (-2.5,0.8) node[above] {$3$};
\path (0.5,0.8) node[above] {$3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
